   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the mental hygiene law, in relation to establishing an\nintensive addiction and medical services integrated services pilot program;\nand providing for the repeal of such provisions upon expiration thereof",
    "basePrintNo": "S7650",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S7650-2021",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [
      {
       "alternate": false,
       "chamber": "SENATE",
       "districtCode": 53,
       "fullName": "Rachel May",
       "imgName": "1057_Rachel_May.jpg",
       "incumbent": true,
       "memberId": 1237,
       "sessionMemberId": 1823,
       "sessionYear": 2021,
       "shortName": "MAY"
      }
     ],
     "size": 1
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Add §19.18-d, Ment Hyg L",
    "lawSection": "Mental Hygiene Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S7650\n\nSPONSOR: HARCKHAM\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the mental hygiene law, in relation to establishing an\nintensive addiction and medical services integrated services pilot\nprogram; and providing for the repeal of such provisions upon expiration\nthereof\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nThis bill would establish an intensive addiction recovery and medical\nintegrated services demonstration program for individuals who have\nsignificant addiction and medical issues\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1: amends the mental hygiene law by adding section 19.18-e to\ncreate a pilot program to support two three-year demonstration programs\nthat provide intensive addiction and medical services integrated\nservices. One of the demonstration programs shall be located in a rural\narea and one shall be located in an urban setting.  Services shall\ninclude but not be limited to intensive physical health care services\n(such as assistance with ADLs, wound care, dialysis, physical therapy\nand occupational therapy), addiction counseling for both the individuals\nand their families, mental health counseling, peer supports, transporta-\ntion assistance, and any other recovery support services that may be\nidentified. Such services shall be primarily provided in an OASAS-certi-\nfied treatment facility, in the community, or in the individual's home,\nas clinically and socially necessitated. Funding may also be used to\nbuild or modify an existing facility.\n\nSection 2: requires the commissioner to provide a written evaluation no\nlater than January first, two thousand twenty-four, and annually there-\nafter, which, at minimum, addresses the overall effectiveness of the\nprogram, identifies best practices for addiction recovery and medical\nintegrated services provided, suggests any additional support services\nthat may be appropriate, and addresses whether continuation or expansion\nof such program is recommended.\n\nSection 3: establishes the effective date\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThe intensive addiction and medical integrated services program is an\ninitiative designed to support individuals suffering from both medical\nconditions and substance use disorders by developing models that remove\nbarriers to accessing the level of care needed. Current addiction treat-\nment centers do not have the ability to treat individuals with medical\nconditions. This demonstration program would create a person-centered\ntreatment model that assists individuals with both sets of diagnoses and\nensures each individual receives a level of care appropriate to them.\nServices encompassed by such a person-centered treatment model may\ninclude:\n\n*Medical Care;\n*Peer Support;\n*Family Counseling/Family Support;\n*Mental Health Counseling;\n*Substance Use Disorder Treatment;\n*Medication Assisted Recovery;\n*Regular employment transportation;\n*Education transportation, such as trade school, 2- or 4-year degree\nprograms, or apprenticeships;\n*Shopping for healthy food, clothing, and other essential items;\n*Trips to Mutual Aid Meetings Narcotics Anonymous/Alcoholics Anonymous;\n*Trips to court or drug court, or court-related appointments (probation\nofficer, social worker, etc.);\n*Trips that reduce social isolation, such as social outings with others\nengaged in sober living; and\n*Trips to local recovery community outreach centers or other community-\nbased supports.\n\nBecause this model takes a wholistic approach, the program will help\nindividuals improve their situation by providing them with hope. The\ncurrent model is not capable of meeting the medical needs of these indi-\nviduals, but the kinds of service offered through the demonstration\nprogram will help someone who would otherwise be denied treatment have\nthe ability to enter treatment, if they choose. The primary purpose of\nthis program is to provide services which are not currently offered to\none of the most vulnerable populations. Giving people the support they\nneed to be healthier by providing integrated services will pay back\ndividends in increased productivity, earning potential, and a decrease\nin New York's overdose fatality rate.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS:\n\nTo be determined.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nApril 1, 2022 to March 31, 2025.\n",
